The Christ god does not like being ignored.  His vengeance is swift and final.	基督之神不喜受人漠視，祂的報應迅速而不容轉圜。
Alas, Mithras is a bad god to offend.  Remember this, in your next life.	唉，米夏拉可不是能輕易冒犯的神祇。這一點，留待你來世牢記吧。
One does not get far when the gods are offended.	觸怒了神明，是走不了多遠的。
Widdershins is a forest sprite.  It must inhabit this shrine.	Widdershins 是森林精靈，想必就棲居在這座神龕裡。
It is an old, bronze statue of Cernunnos and he holds out a cup for offerings.	這是一尊古老的青銅像，塑的是 Cernunnos，祂伸出雙手捧著一只杯子，等候供品。
Ah!  Of course!  The idol is bronze.  That explains Widdershins's toll of copper or tin.	啊！這就對了！這尊神像是青銅所鑄，難怪 Widdershins 收的過路費，非銅即錫。
This shrine contains a bronze statue of the Celtic god, Cernunnos, within a stone housing.	這座石造神龕裡，供奉著凱爾特神祇 Cernunnos 的青銅像。
'Tis a rock.	這是一塊岩石。
'Tis a tree.	這是一棵樹。
'Tis a bush.	這是一叢灌木。
The forest sprite is not a man.	這森林精靈並非凡人。
There is no one here.	此處空無一人。
The miserable sprite has turned everything but your copper coins into pebbles!	這可惡的精靈把你身上除了銅幣之外的東西全變成了石子！
Your copper coins are untouched by the spell.	你的銅幣未受此咒波及。
I would worry less about the pebbles than about how to get Widdershins to undo his trick.	與其擔心那些石子，不如想想該如何讓 Widdershins 收回這個惡作劇。
You have more important things to worry about than pebbles.	比起石子，你有更要緊的事該操心。
Mercifully, he is gone.	所幸，他已經離開了。
Widdershins only wants a bit of copper.  I would not be stingy, if I were you.	Widdershins 要的不過是一點銅幣。若我是你，可不會這麼小氣。
Try not to disturb Widdershins again.	盡量別再驚動 Widdershins 了。
The mischievous forest sprite, Widdershins, is perched upon the shrine, waiting for you to pay his toll.	頑皮的森林精靈 Widdershins 蹲踞在神龕上，等著你繳納過路費。
It should look familiar.  This is where you entered the forest and there is the little shrine.	這裡你該不陌生。你就是從這裡踏入森林的，那座小神龕也在此處。
Let us hope Widdershins is asleep.	但願 Widdershins 正在沉睡。
You must pass through the Forest Perilous to reach Glastonbury Tor.  The forest is so named because of the many dangers hidden within.	你必須穿越派瑞樂斯森林，才能抵達格列斯登伯利山。這座森林之所以如此命名，正因其中潛藏著重重危機。
In this clearing, there is a very old forest shrine.	這片林間空地上，立著一座古老的森林神龕。
He has returned to his hiding place inside the shrine.	他已退回神龕裡的藏身之處。
It is empty	此處空無一物
The statue of Cernunnos has antlers, representing the fertility of the stag.	Cernunnos 的塑像頭生鹿角，象徵雄鹿的豐饒生機。
Cernunnos is an ancient Celtic god of the hunt or sometimes of the underworld.	Cernunnos 是凱爾特人古老的狩獵之神，有時也被視為冥界之神。
The bronze statue is of the Celtic god, Cernunnos.	這尊青銅像塑的是凱爾特神祇 Cernunnos。
If he passed this way, he left no traces.	縱使他曾路過此地，也未留下任何痕跡。
It is not very powerful, but such sprites are quite mischievous and will play small, but nasty tricks upon those who displease them.	他法力不算高強，但這類精靈生性頑皮，遇上惹惱自己的人，便會使出些雖小卻惡毒的把戲。
The idol is bronze and bronze is an alloy made of copper and tin.  Widdershins has become obsessed, I would say, with making bronze.	這尊神像是青銅所鑄，青銅乃銅與錫合煉而成的合金。依我看，Widdershins 是著了鑄造青銅的魔。
It does not speak.  It does not even hear.	它不會說話，甚至聽不見。
You have none to give.	你身上並無可以奉上之物。
Get closer to the front of the shrine.	再靠近神龕正面一些。
You will never see that money again.	那筆錢，你是再也見不著了。
Ah, how pleasant.  He has gone away for now.	啊，真是清靜。他這會兒已經走了。
That is of no use here.	此物在此毫無用處。
You have only just entered the Forest Perilous. That can wait until later.  For now, be on your guard.	你才剛踏入派瑞樂斯森林，那事不妨稍後再議。眼下，還是提高警覺為要。
You cannot really harm such an elemental creature with mere force.	單憑蠻力，你傷不了這種元素精怪。
Besides, you would have to catch it first and that is nearly impossible.	再說，你得先逮住牠才行，而那幾乎是不可能的事。
I could do so with a spell, but only if I were there in person.  You will never do so otherwise.	我倒是能施法辦到，但得親身在場才行。否則，你是絕無可能做到的。
You have not the means to shatter stone or break bronze.  Besides, the shrine is holy and not to blame that Widdershins has taken up residence in it.	你沒有能耐擊碎岩石或毀壞青銅。再說，這神龕本是聖地，Widdershins 選中此處棲身，也怪不得它。
You will find it is too heavy and solidly rooted in the ground.	你會發現它重不可舉，且深深扎根於地。
You cannot harm a sprite with mere steel.	區區鋼鐵，傷不了精靈。
Copper or tin! Copper or tin! Copper or tin for Widdershins!	銅也好，錫也好！銅也好，錫也好！Widdershins 只要銅或錫！
Single-minded creature, is he not?	這傢伙倒是心無旁騖，是吧？
The sprite has struck!  He has afflicted you with an attack of stinging ants!	精靈出手了！他放出一群螫人的螞蟻襲擊你！
By my beard!  You no sooner put the copper in the shrine than the sprite made off with it.	我的鬍子啊！你才剛把銅幣放進神龕，那精靈轉眼便捲著它跑了。
Ah, did you feel a tingling?  The trick has been undone, but check your purse, if you wish to make sure.	啊，你感覺到一陣刺麻了嗎？這惡作劇已經解除，但你若想確認，不妨查看一下錢囊。
There is no sign of the sprite, yet you know he must stil be about.	精靈不見蹤影，但你明白他必定仍在附近。
Copper is mine! Copper divine! Go your way, No trick I will play.	銅幣歸我，銅幣是寶！你儘管上路，惡作劇到此為止。
Silver and gold do not pay my toll! Copper or tin is my only whim!	金銀寶物付不了我的過路費！我只鍾情銅與錫！
You found nothing, of course.	你自然是什麼也沒找到。
Do make up your mind.	拿定主意吧。
It is a forest sprite.  It must inhabit this shrine.	這是一名森林精靈，想必就棲居在這座神龕裡。
Copper or tin!  Keep your useless human stuff.  Copper or tin!	銅也好，錫也好！你那些沒用的凡人玩意兒，留著自己用吧。銅也好，錫也好！
Aye, copper!  Give me copper!	是的，銅幣！把銅幣給我！
My toll you pay or a trick I play!	繳了過路費，不然就等著挨我的把戲！
Going out or coming in, Copper or tin for Widdershins! You had better heed my whim, Pay the toll of Widdershins! If no copper or no tin, You get a trick from Widdershins!	不論是出是進，Widdershins 都要銅或錫！你最好順著我的心意，繳清 Widdershins 的過路費！若是沒銅也沒錫，Widdershins 就讓你嚐嚐把戲！
Why would you want to do that?	你何必這麼做？
There is a spear leaning on the rack behind the Hunter.	獵人身後的架子上，斜倚著一支長矛。
From the simplicity of the Hunter's clothing and his worn face, I would say that he is a poor man.	從獵人樸素的衣著與滿是風霜的面容看來，我猜他是個窮苦人。
It is a makeshift rack.	這是個臨時搭成的架子。
The hunter has several pelts drying upon simple racks made of sticks.	獵人用幾根木棍搭成簡陋的架子，上頭晾著幾張獸皮。
Did you expect the spear to be here?  You were warned that it could not leave the forest.	你以為長矛會在這裡？早有人警告過你，它離不開這座森林。
The spear remains wedged in the carcass of the boar.	那支長矛仍卡在野豬的屍身裡。
He is clearly not here.	他顯然不在這裡。
It would be best to avoid the creature, unless you have copper or tin to give it.	除非你備有銅或錫可以奉上，否則最好避開這東西。
In this small clearing, a Hunter has set up his skins on racks to dry.  Next to him are three spears.  He is a bold man or a desperate one to seek his living in the Forest Perilous.	這片小小的空地上，一名獵人架起木架晾曬獸皮，身旁擺著三支長矛。能在派瑞樂斯森林裡討生活，這人不是膽識過人，就是走投無路。
It is the same as when you passed through here before.  The Hunter is still here with his spears and his skins.	這裡的景象和你先前經過時一樣。獵人依舊守在這裡，帶著他的長矛與獸皮。
This clearing seems larger without the Hunter in it.	少了獵人，這片空地似乎顯得開闊了些。
The Hunter has left and taken everything with him.	獵人已經離去，並帶走了他所有的家當。
Like a true man of the forest, he has vanished leaving no trace that he was ever here.	一如真正的林中人，他消失得無影無蹤，彷彿從未來過此地。
There is no lance here, nor did the Hunter have one.	這裡沒有長槍，獵人本來也不曾有過。
Your best source of such information, the Hunter, is now gone.	你打聽此事最好的線索——那位獵人，如今已經離開了。
There is no use for it here.	此物在此派不上用場。
If you are threatened by a boar, that will be the time to make use of it.	若遭野豬威脅，正是用上它的時候。
You cannot use what you do not have.	你不曾擁有之物，如何使用？
You are talking nonsense.	你這是在胡言亂語。
I am sure you will find a likely bush somewhere in the forest for that.	我想，森林中不乏合適的灌木叢可供此用。
I know who you are, Arthur.	我知道你是誰，亞瑟。
I did not know that trees could pay taxes.	我倒不知道樹木也得納稅。
There is no one here save you and the mule.	此處除了你與騾子，別無他人。
From whom? There is no-one here.	向誰要？這裡空無一人。
Difficult, since he is not here.	這可難了，畢竟他人不在這裡。
He is no longer here, though a trace of him does linger on.	他已不在此處，但仍留下幾分蹤跡。
But the Hunter is gone.	但獵人已經離開了。
Is something wrong, m'lord?	有什麼不對勁嗎，吾王？
I sense no danger here, but you are wise to be at readiness at all times in this forest.	我未感應到此處有何危險，但你時時保持戒備，在這座森林裡是明智之舉。
The staff of this boar spear is made of the sacred oak and the spearhead forged by the Black Knight himself.	這支獵豬矛的矛桿取自聖橡木，矛頭則由黑武士親手鍛造。
I will fasten it beneath your steed's trappings, so it will be secure until needed.	我會將它繫在你坐騎的鞍具下方，妥善收好，待用時取出。
'Tis done.  But be warned, m'lord, the spear draws its power from the Forest itself and cannot be taken away from it.	辦妥了。但吾王須知，這支長矛的力量源自森林本身，不能被帶離此地。
I am called Hugo the Bold.	人稱我作「無畏雨果」。
Ask the Hunter.	去問那獵人吧。
The Hunter would likely know more than I.	獵人知道的，多半比我還多。
This boar spear once belonged to the Black Knight and is powerful enough to kill even the giant boars of this Forest.  He has laid an enchantment on it so that it belongs to the Forest and cannot be taken from here.	這支獵豬矛曾屬於黑武士所有，威力足以誅殺這座森林裡的巨型野豬。他在矛上施了法術，使它歸屬於森林，無法帶離此地。
We are very poor and I have five children to feed.	我們家境貧寒，還有五個孩子要養活。
I have no lances, only my spears.	我沒有長槍，只有這些長矛。
You see the results of many weary days of work, m'lord -- hare, fox and squirrel pelts.	吾王您瞧，這是我多少個勞苦日子的成果——野兔、狐狸與松鼠的毛皮。
I notch together branches and sticks and use them for drying skins.	我把樹枝與木棍刻榫拼接，用來晾曬獸皮。
That is a most unhappy looking mule you have, m'lord.	吾王您這頭騾子，瞧著可真是愁眉苦臉。
I fear the danger of the forest was too much for it. That is one reason I never bring a pack animal with me.	恐怕是這森林裡的凶險嚇壞了牠。這也是我從不帶馱獸同行的原因之一。
Aye, it is a noxious pest.  I give it bits of tin to keep it happy.	是啊，那是個惹人厭的討厭鬼。我拿些碎錫餵牠，好讓牠消停。
Cernunnos is the God of the Hunt.	Cernunnos 乃狩獵之神。
Even one copper coin would be of much use to me, sire, and help greatly to loosen my tongue.	哪怕一枚銅幣，對我都大有用處，陛下，也能讓我這張嘴更樂意開口。
The forest is full of terrible dangers. I survive by remaining carefully upon the outskirts of it.	這座森林充滿可怕的危險。我之所以能活下來，全靠謹慎地待在林緣一帶。
He is a part of this forest, binding its power to him with dark magicks.  You must face his challenge, m'lord, if you wish to reach the Tor.	他已與這座森林融為一體，以黑暗法術將其力量繫於己身。吾王若想抵達那座山，就必須迎戰他的挑戰。
Which knight, m'lord?	是哪一位騎士，吾王？
Aye, that goodly knight came this way and rode on into the Forest to reach the Tor where he hoped to find the Grail.	是的，那位良善的騎士確實經過此地，策馬深入森林，欲往山上尋找聖杯。
But I fear he has met an evil fate, for he did not pass this way again.	但恐怕他已遭逢不幸，因為此後再也不曾見他經過此地。
I know nothing of him, m'lord.	我對他一無所知，吾王。
The local legend goes that Joseph of Arimathea brought the holy cup here from Jerusalem and hid it upon Glastonbury Tor.  That is all I know of it.	當地流傳的傳說是，亞利馬太的約瑟從耶路撒冷將聖杯帶到此地，藏於格列斯登伯利山上。我所知的僅止於此。
I would not dare to search there myself, because of the Mad Monk.	我自己是不敢前去搜尋的，因為那瘋僧侶在那裡盤據。
Sire, a strange monk has taken up residence in the ruins atop Glastonbury Tor.  He has surrounded the base of the Tor with a sorcerous barrier and stones that speak!	陛下，一名古怪的僧侶已在格列斯登伯利山頂的廢墟中住了下來。他在山腳布下了法術屏障，還有會說話的石頭！
The giant boars of this forest are especially dangerous.  Only a boar spear of the greatest strength is effective against them.	這座森林裡的巨型野豬格外凶險，唯有最強韌的獵豬矛才能制服牠們。
Aye, there is a dreadful hag that turns men to stone. Beware of her.	是的，這裡有個可怕的老巫婆，能把人變成石頭。務必提防她。
The bones of knights vanquished by the Black Knight are often left in the forest as a warning.  I pray yours will not be amongst them.	那些被黑武士擊敗的騎士，屍骨常被棄置林間以示警戒。我祈求您的骨骸不會添列其中。
Nothing I can tell you about that would be of value to you, m'lord.	關於此事，我實在說不出什麼對吾王有用的話。
What a mean and petty demand for a king to make. Leave such venal matters to your tax collectors.	這等卑劣瑣碎的要求，豈是一位君王該提的？這類貪鄙之事，就交給你的稅吏去辦吧。
Forgive me, m'lord, but the tax collector came by last week and took my last copper coin.	恕罪，吾王，稅吏上週才來過，把我最後一枚銅幣也收走了。
Leave that to the tax collectors, Arthur.  It is far beneath your dignity to do such a job.	這種事就交給稅吏去做吧，亞瑟。親自過問，實在有失你的身分。
A strange word, m'lord.  I do not understand its meaning.	這詞真怪，吾王。我不明白它的意思。
Tis an honor to be acknowledged by you, m'lord.	承蒙吾王垂顧，實乃莫大榮幸。
Aye, m'lord.  So I judged from your royal bearing, magnificent horse, and the symbol of Pendragon upon your shield.	正是，吾王。我是從您那王者的氣度、雄駿的坐騎，還有盾牌上潘德拉貢的紋章看出來的。
Having spoken with Sir Gawaine, I know something of what you seek.  You have but to ask and I will gladly share what I know, especially about the Mad Monk of Glastonbury and the Grail.	我曾與高文爵士交談過，因此略知您所尋為何物。您只管開口相問，我必樂意告知所知一切，尤其是格列斯登伯利的瘋僧侶與聖杯之事。
I am but a poor man, m'lord, but perhaps in some small way I may be of service to you.  Aye, tis a hard life in the forest to earn a few thin coins to feed my family.	我不過是個窮苦人，吾王，但或許還能盡些微薄之力為您效勞。是啊，在這森林裡討生活著實艱苦，為了幾枚薄幣養家糊口。
He is a good fellow, but smells a bit gamy.	他是個好人，只是身上有股獵物的腥氣。
From the impoverished look of him, that might best be accomplished by giving him money.	從他窮困的模樣看來，給他些錢財，或許是最好的辦法。
You have no cause to attack his man.  If you are angry for some reason, look to your own faults and remember that a king must set an example by his behavior.	你沒有理由攻擊他的人。若你有什麼緣故動怒，該先反省自身的過失，別忘了君王須以身作則。
